</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define transverse intersection.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>What does</p>

<p>
<md>A\pitchfork B</md>
</p>

<p>mean?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define an orientable manifold.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a manifold with boundary.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define an isotopy.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a Morse function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define a nondegenerate critical point.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define the Morse index.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the Morse Lemma.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>What is an <m>n</m>-dimensional handle of index <m>\lambda</m>?</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define cobordism.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that</p>

<p>
<md>S^1
=
\{(x,y)\in\R^2:x^2+y^2=1\}</md>
</p>

<p>is a smooth submanifold of <m>\R^2</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that</p>

<p>
<md>S^2
=
\{(x,y,z)\in\R^3:x^2+y^2+z^2=1\}</md>
</p>

<p>is a smooth submanifold of <m>\R^3</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether <m>0</m> is a regular value of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether <m>1</m> is a regular value of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the critical points of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the critical points of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x,y)=x^2-y^2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the Morse index of the critical point of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x,y)=x^2-y^2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Classify the critical point of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2</md>
</p>

<p>using the Hessian.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Classify the critical point of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x,y)=-x^2-y^2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why a transverse intersection of two surfaces in a
three-dimensional manifold is generically one-dimensional.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>A^3,B^4\subseteq M^6</md>
</p>

<p>intersect transversely, determine</p>

<p>
<md>\dim(A\cap B).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why perpendicular submanifolds are transverse but transverse
submanifolds need not be perpendicular.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the degree of</p>

<p>
<md>f:S^1\to S^1,
\qquad
f(z)=z^5.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the degree of</p>

<p>
<md>f:S^1\to S^1,
\qquad
f(z)=z^{-3}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why a map of nonzero degree between compact connected oriented
manifolds of equal dimension must be surjective.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why the Hairy Ball Theorem applies to <m>S^2</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Construct a nowhere-zero tangent vector field on <m>S^1</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Verify</p>

<p>
<md>\chi(S^2)=2</md>
</p>

<p>and explain its relationship with the Poincar\'e--Hopf Theorem.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For a standard height function on <m>S^2</m>, list the critical points and
their Morse indices.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For a standard height function on a torus, list the four critical-point
indices.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the Morse critical points of the torus to calculate its Euler
characteristic.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why isotopy is stronger than homotopy.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Give an example of two maps that may be homotopic even though a proposed
deformation through embeddings would be impossible.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that a diffeomorphism is a homeomorphism.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the inclusion</p>

<p>
<md>S^n\hookrightarrow\R^{n+1}</md>
</p>

<p>is an immersion.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the projection</p>

<p>
<md>\pi:\R^{m+n}\to\R^n</md>
</p>

<p>onto the final <m>n</m> coordinates is a submersion.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the Regular Value Theorem to show that</p>

<p>
<md>\{(x,y,z)\in\R^3:x^2+y^2-z^2=1\}</md>
</p>

<p>is a smooth surface.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find all critical values of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>f(x,y)=x^3-3x+y^2,</md>
</p>

<p>find the critical points and determine whether each is nondegenerate.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine the Morse index of each nondegenerate critical point in the
previous exercise.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain how the Constant Rank Theorem contains the local behavior of
immersions and submersions as special cases.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that if <m>f:M\to N</m> is transverse to a point <m>y\in N</m>, then <m>y</m>
is a regular value.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>A^a,B^b\subseteq M^n</md>
</p>

<p>intersect transversely. Derive the dimension formula</p>

<p>
<md>\dim(A\cap B)=a+b-n.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why generic perturbations are useful when defining intersection
numbers.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that</p>

<p>
<md>\partial(\partial M)=\varnothing</md>
</p>

<p>for familiar examples of manifolds with boundary.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute the degree of the antipodal map</p>

<p>
<md>A:S^n\to S^n,
\qquad
A(x)=-x.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the degree of the antipodal map is</p>

<p>
<md>(-1)^{n+1}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why degree is invariant under homotopy.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the Poincar\'e--Hopf theorem to explain why a compact manifold with
nonzero Euler characteristic cannot admit a nowhere-zero vector field.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For a Morse function on a compact surface, explain how index-<m>0</m>,
index-<m>1</m>, and index-<m>2</m> critical points alter the topology of sublevel
sets.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Verify the weak Morse inequalities for <m>S^2</m> using its standard height
function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Verify the weak Morse inequalities for <m>T^2</m> using a generic height
function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain how Morse theory gives a handle decomposition of a compact smooth
manifold.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that</p>

<p>
<md>S^1\times[0,1]</md>
</p>

<p>is a cobordism between two circles.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Study the proof of the Whitney Embedding Theorem and explain why the
dimension <m>2n</m> appears.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Study Sard's Theorem and explain why it is essential in degree theory.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Research the Parametric Transversality Theorem.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use transversality to explain why two generic curves in a surface intersect
in isolated points.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use transversality to explain why two generic surfaces in a
three-dimensional manifold intersect along curves.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Research the relationship between intersection number and homology.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Research one differential-topological proof of the Brouwer Fixed-Point
Theorem.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Research the relationship between degree theory and the Fundamental Theorem
of Algebra.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Study the proof of the Hairy Ball Theorem using degree or homology.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Research the proof of the Poincar\'e--Hopf Theorem.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Construct a Morse function on the torus and analyze its critical points.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Research the strong Morse inequalities.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain how a Morse function gives a CW decomposition of a manifold.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Research Morse homology and describe how its boundary operator is defined.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Research exotic spheres and explain the distinction between homeomorphism
and diffeomorphism that they demonstrate.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Research exotic <m>\R^4</m> and explain why dimension four is exceptional in
smooth topology.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Investigate cobordism groups and explain how disjoint union defines their
addition.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-03-differential-topology-subsec-differential-topology-summary">
<title>Conceptual Summary</title>
<p>Differential topology studies smooth manifolds and smooth maps while
ignoring metric quantities such as length and curvature.</p>

<p>The differential</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
df_p:T_pM\to T_{f(p)}N
}</md>
</p>

<p>encodes the local linear behavior of a smooth map.</p>

<p>An immersion has injective differential.</p>

<p>A submersion has surjective differential.</p>

<p>An embedding is an immersion that is also a homeomorphism onto its image.</p>

<p>Regular values generate smooth submanifolds:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\dim f^{-1}(y)
=
\dim M-\dim N.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Sard's Theorem shows that critical values form a measure-zero set.</p>

<p>Transversality generalizes regularity and makes intersections behave with
the expected dimension:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\dim(A\cap B)
=
\dim A+\dim B-\dim M.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Orientation allows intersections and preimages to be counted with signs.</p>

<p>For maps between compact connected oriented manifolds of equal dimension,
these signs produce the degree:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\deg(f)
=
\sum_{x\in f^{-1}(y)}
\operatorname{sgn}(df_x).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Vector fields connect local differential behavior with global topology
through</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sum_p\operatorname{ind}_p(X)
=
\chi(M).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Morse theory studies manifolds using critical points of functions</p>

<p>
<md>f:M\to\R.</md>
</p>

<p>At a nondegenerate critical point,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f
=
f(p)
-
x_1^2-\cdots-x_\lambda^2
+
x_{\lambda+1}^2+\cdots+x_n^2
}</md>
</p>

<p>in suitable local coordinates.</p>

<p>Passing a critical value corresponds to attaching a handle</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
D^\lambda\times D^{n-\lambda}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The numbers of Morse critical points are constrained by homology through the
Morse inequalities.</p>

<p>Cobordism studies manifolds that arise together as boundaries of
higher-dimensional manifolds.</p>

<p>Finally, exotic smooth structures demonstrate that smooth topology can
contain information invisible to ordinary topology.</p>

<p>Thus differential topology creates the bridge</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{calculus}
\longleftrightarrow
\text{smooth manifolds}
\longleftrightarrow
\text{global topology}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-03-differential-topology-subsec-differential-topology-connections">
<title>Section Connections</title>
<remark>
<title>Chapter Connection</title>
<p>Differential Topology builds directly on Point-Set Topology and Algebraic
Topology while using the smooth-manifold language introduced in Differential
Geometry. The Regular Value Theorem and Transversality Theorem explain how
smooth submanifolds and intersections arise. Degree theory connects smooth
maps with homology and orientation. The Poincar\'e--Hopf Theorem connects
vector fields with Euler characteristic. Morse Theory converts critical-point
data into global topological information and provides handle decompositions
of manifolds. Isotopy provides the natural language for embedded objects and
leads directly into Knot Theory. Cobordism and smooth structures become
central in higher-dimensional manifold topology. These ideas prepare the
reader for Geometric Topology and the dedicated study of Manifolds that
follows.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>

</section>
